#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02b4e90d-c970-54c3-ab06-a30ba09864e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278607} {:answer/id #uuid "7ebe8543-ec3e-587e-9768-a831766e3eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279734} {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} {:answer/id #uuid "acad9a07-0f5d-5457-86b1-82efec638ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280120} {:answer/id #uuid "ea617dc5-3c2b-587f-bfa4-9046fb77867d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa98d390-38c9-5242-9163-03ea126a9877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280105} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ee8cb1-a891-5ada-b9cf-ae248d766e50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152cbc4e-4f16-538e-9423-127439b0fd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162408" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c423938-da5c-5986-904d-ca90bf43b04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278710} {:answer/id #uuid "1603c0df-af5e-5636-b1a6-72475e40234a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278787} {:answer/id #uuid "1d02c2fc-86f7-5eda-b6b6-62da495ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278860} {:answer/id #uuid "4b1ed5c0-e3de-599b-b026-5cb574362292" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279264} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc2db2a-7618-540a-a2a1-b6f665e7242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{800\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d02c2fc-86f7-5eda-b6b6-62da495ea6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278860} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9ce83a8-eea0-5bfa-a466-b577953f924a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088370e5-5be7-5c78-9dd1-180055943e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-162410" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d80dd4e3-7ed1-503d-9671-7f45280ca24f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130985} {:answer/id #uuid "17a086d8-d007-5821-8115-e76ddac0915a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130986} {:answer/id #uuid "4a19f710-b32c-5035-a13b-72785acbdf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130987} {:answer/id #uuid "67b10b1f-2e11-5777-9ba4-8b1485780761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130988} {:answer/id #uuid "755be662-4415-5c52-965a-228af18f717c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130989}] :answer-field/id #uuid "4a64fc7d-ca43-526d-a9de-b6748f05a24c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60179c77-308c-5a4b-9b41-da233c3efd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374181} {:answer/id #uuid "c11f9a42-43be-517a-94cc-42c6ba595526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374182} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d48b0f-7937-5ff0-8ff5-b0a889036dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374183} {:answer/id #uuid "ae328f95-387f-55bc-b6b1-78da1a0e6c8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374184} {:answer/id #uuid "9829f33d-f493-59a1-b8be-a91cbaec38dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60179c77-308c-5a4b-9b41-da233c3efd4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374181} :answer-field/id #uuid "a29f5580-8460-5ca2-bf68-68db1fc8ae7a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de14125f-fde2-50fc-9770-aae06e6d9c30" :question/math-academy-id "q-162411" :question/problem "![](images/d0/d0f6f5ed91981e15d2fb80e2630cec6d20f566feb627899f529e07bab12b73fb.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨{z}^{2},\\,2y,\\,{x}^{2}+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+y) \\\\  & =0+2+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a718cce9-8e5d-5dec-8a0a-3840ae5b697b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374195} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dec189-d251-5cdb-beb7-7e7b56595d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374196} {:answer/id #uuid "1af109f7-1025-5d70-baae-5d18a7702281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374197} {:answer/id #uuid "a2591ccc-8c08-599f-b2a7-b656cee9d9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374198} {:answer/id #uuid "0653f4e3-f779-564a-8100-ee0e0831159c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2dec189-d251-5cdb-beb7-7e7b56595d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374196} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fbd410-95a8-5da8-9827-3827f59a3a95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a61b4d5a-a480-5473-9f9f-c9865eee4653" :question/math-academy-id "q-162571" :question/problem "![](images/07/07b46ab683e54813e0f6090c6841302ea7d914bc6b1577afc883f6e42e0a3903.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x-y{z}^{2},\\,y+{e}^{z},\\,2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3+1+0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d047b1e-3afb-5c5a-88f9-faeab4432ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279129} {:answer/id #uuid "565cbac4-9d5b-53f7-ad95-f3c76b6932d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279384} {:answer/id #uuid "93da9f2a-8ea0-53d4-858e-491166b1dc48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279908} {:answer/id #uuid "a5861e93-47f0-536a-a4e4-9d3f7a9b658a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280057} {:answer/id #uuid "da4056b9-a344-5551-a7d2-55af24530e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d047b1e-3afb-5c5a-88f9-faeab4432ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279129} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b030670-12c0-5a90-a682-f7582e669ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c50889a1-249a-5104-a142-d8d313afba10" :question/math-academy-id "q-162572" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133930 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3274b94e-9050-5914-92cf-a20e7f33b9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279033} {:answer/id #uuid "4021cef0-295e-5798-9f4f-2157ebca2b8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279158} {:answer/id #uuid "59d4c631-de3a-5f80-abfd-ae0e4830d3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279414} {:answer/id #uuid "659a3952-f331-5c0f-9621-9a4212215836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279515} {:answer/id #uuid "a356390a-20fd-5203-b264-de87732ef03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "659a3952-f331-5c0f-9621-9a4212215836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279515} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6afff7-29bd-5084-9d8e-63605244a213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9b12219-fd15-591a-8410-7eb56a488ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-162578" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $F=z\\,i+6y\\,j+(x+3z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+3z) \\\\  & =0+6+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}9\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133929 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "100640c1-bb74-5261-b07b-162818bcec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278736} {:answer/id #uuid "612e775d-f4d3-5491-a568-8632b1dc20b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279479} {:answer/id #uuid "a3e024e7-b5ac-5532-a30e-fcebd4a57c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280046} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3f43b4-3268-56c7-b7af-4bdd5b0709c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280255} {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c001c748-078c-5145-9bb6-76a3e6bc31f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280282} :answer-field/id #uuid "78ab3a06-7407-5d2e-b3fb-5448f190ff8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34056bf8-ebf0-50cf-86bc-5d67c31ea40b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162588" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430948 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f5be7e-0bfc-59a7-af3a-6dfcf5d7d844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430913} {:answer/id #uuid "23e226ce-01b7-5120-bfc5-5949d300bfcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430916} {:answer/id #uuid "475b50f4-a66e-582c-bb09-b18513917dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430921} {:answer/id #uuid "e03f06d7-09f6-5991-8022-a31e044488bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430935} {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecbcc9d3-a1f4-5e44-b6a6-513ff6a3a28b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430938} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc27d6bf-860e-505c-82f5-df4b985c7b2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09c354f4-ffe0-5d80-b23d-97b7671b8b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-162595" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=-1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y{z}^{2}\\,i-2yz\\,j+3{z}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2z)+6z \\\\  & =4z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{2}4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [2{z}^{2}]_{z=-1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot (8-2) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537916 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57dc0f26-61e8-54f6-8765-f4f9dd4d0d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537408} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2113a2-dd4d-539c-984a-b4939769ed41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537521} {:answer/id #uuid "c491e7f1-cd3a-54c5-a9fb-12f4835abac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537606} {:answer/id #uuid "e023c4cd-5ab4-51c0-8881-2596a66eaf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537680} {:answer/id #uuid "e98c24e5-1c22-5064-93fa-bfdbc05fdb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a2113a2-dd4d-539c-984a-b4939769ed41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537521} :answer-field/id #uuid "69fd017c-8fd8-594c-96db-bb6def0c9f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537803}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "56f4d79a-05af-516b-82f5-f4f41e31d95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162598" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=\\pi ,$ $z=2,$ $z=4.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={z}^{3}\\,i+2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+x{y}^{2}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{3})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =0+(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+0 \\\\  & =-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}(-2xz\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{z=2}^{z=4} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0)\\cdot (\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcc0510-4039-55e0-823c-e4ac7d5653d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374210} {:answer/id #uuid "0acfab67-9718-5e64-b967-a1914c71eddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374211} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf62725-3d23-5464-bf59-4f18a980c4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374212} {:answer/id #uuid "702faaa4-c282-5398-860f-c6e1f3c9bb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ced5422-0477-54ea-a063-de1490b4e5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2a8924c-323c-54ea-b830-8522b9835da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162602" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(2{x}^{3}+z)\\,i-z\\,j-3{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-3{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+0+(-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28fa7678-f13a-5850-9ebd-3577ac1a0379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537311} {:answer/id #uuid "ae79307e-0a76-5408-ab09-a497ed81c568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537560} {:answer/id #uuid "b591f2b8-be38-5e17-9192-b6351f2a138c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537579} {:answer/id #uuid "d60c91b7-6822-585c-88c2-65de5a82f0ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537654} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc29b15-264e-5331-b92c-70ab6eedaf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbc29b15-264e-5331-b92c-70ab6eedaf01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537669} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b6833fc-e197-5043-abac-1f89f7ffca8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46a23183-289b-59ee-a9e5-bbe0aa79406a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162603" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=4,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\sqrt{x}\\,i+(z-x)\\,j+{y}^{3}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\sqrt{x})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{y}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\sqrt{x}]_{x=1}^{x=4}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(2-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133931 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24ea0e2b-5e7a-52c9-a1b3-f61827c82bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430917} {:answer/id #uuid "676e7034-5bd6-59ee-bc64-fafa0a12a224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430926} {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5308bb-1224-54c9-a249-060024986a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430934} {:answer/id #uuid "fa91438f-d562-5478-84cb-9cc0479a64c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "727b00ee-627c-530d-87da-873160a2d3d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430930} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f84ddbb-0ce0-5549-8bb9-1dfef0b987f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ce23f40-f256-54cd-9b13-888ae9d3dff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-162605" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=3,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y+{z}^{2})\\,i-x\\,j+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y+{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}) \\\\  & =0+0+3{x}^{2}{e}^{z} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{x}^{2}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot [{e}^{z}]_{z=0}^{z=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-{0}^{3})\\cdot 2\\cdot ({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
